  3. What the shown code does establish works against the claim: the deviation check is a fail-safe. If the pool spot
  price diverges from the Chainlink base price, the oracle refuses to answer rather than returning a manipulated LP
  value. That is the intended purpose of passing _lowerLimitMultiplier/_upperLimitMultiplier into the constructor at
  all, and of probing get_dy (fee-inclusive, therefore harder to round-trip profitably) instead of get_virtual_price or
  price_oracle. Trading a temporary revert for a wrong price is a deliberate design decision visible in the constructor
  surface, not an unguarded flaw. The comment "The primary index spot price is left as zero" plus the fee-inclusive
  probe show the deviation machinery is the intended safety property being exercised.
  4. The exploit economics as stated do not close either. The griefer must, every single block until "deeply insolvent,"
  (a) win top-of-block ordering, (b) pay Curve's swap fee on ~13% of TVL in both directions, and (c) absorb the risk
  that any third party arbitrages the imbalance mid-block, which restores the price and re-enables liquidation.
  Meanwhile the position keeps accruing the very insolvency that makes the attacker's own collateral worth less. This is
  the generic "oracle deviation guard can be griefed into temporary DoS" pattern, which is a recognized and accepted
  tradeoff of bounded-deviation LP oracles, not a break of an invariant in this file.
  5. No step in the shown code breaks an invariant for an unprivileged caller. The only mutable-by-attacker input is
